\begin{didyouknow}{Turing Named the Problem}
Alan Turing introduced the term \emph{condition number} in a 1948 paper on rounding errors in matrix computations. It measures how much a matrix can amplify small errors in the data. A famous troublemaker is the Hilbert matrix, with entries $1/(i+j-1)$. The $10\times10$ version has a condition number around $10^{13}$, so solving with it can wipe out most of a computer's digits.
\end{didyouknow}
